WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Docker basics.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.735
Works on my machine stops being an excuse once your app ships with its own machine.

3
00:00:06.735 --> 00:00:07.500
Let's see how.

4
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.100
Two words to get straight.

5
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.500
An image is an immutable, layered snapshot of a filesystem, built from a Dockerfile.

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.867
A container is a running instance of that image, with a thin writable layer on top.

7
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:23.733
Kill the container and the image is untouched.

8
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:25.744
So the image is the machine.

9
00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:28.067
That is what ends works on my machine.

10
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:31.808
Now the daily-life skill: layer caching.

11
00:00:31.808 --> 00:00:34.667
Every Dockerfile instruction creates a layer.

12
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:36.700
At the bottom, the base image.

13
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.467
Then the requirements file.

14
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.667
Then the slow dependency install.

15
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:42.685
And on top, your source code.

16
00:00:42.685 --> 00:00:46.300
A layer rebuilds only if it, or anything beneath it, changed.

17
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:48.667
Here is the payoff.

18
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:50.433
You edit one line of code.

19
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:55.100
Everything beneath your code is cached, so dependencies are not reinstalled.

20
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.133
Only the code layer rebuilds.

21
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.887
Three seconds, instead of three minutes.

22
00:00:59.887 --> 00:01:02.033
Put what changes most often on top.

23
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:04.567
Here is the Dockerfile.

24
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:10.000
We copy the requirements file and install dependencies first, since they rarely change.

25
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.667
Only then do we copy the source code, which changes daily.

26
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:18.300
Rebuild after an edit, and every layer says cached, except the code copy.

27
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.733
Picture a live demo where you are fixing prompt wording between sessions.

28
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:28.242
With the naive Dockerfile, every one-line tweak reinstalls torch.

29
00:01:28.242 --> 00:01:29.667
Three-minute rebuilds.

30
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:33.767
The fix is to install dependencies before copying the source.

31
00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:36.210
Now each rebuild takes three seconds.

32
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.933
The order of two lines is the whole difference.

33
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:40.897
Let's recap.

34
00:01:40.897 --> 00:01:43.633
An image is an immutable, layered snapshot.

35
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:46.267
A container is a running instance of it.

36
00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:50.033
A layer rebuilds only if it, or a layer beneath it, changed.

37
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:53.872
So put slow, stable steps first and your code last.

38
00:01:53.872 --> 00:01:56.300
Next up: Docker multi-stage builds.
